.TH MEMORY.HPP 3v32 "November 23, 2026" "v32c++ 20261123-dev" "v32c++ Library"
.SH NAME
v32/memory.hpp \- the arena allocator and the garbage collector of v32c++ (--alloc=arena, --alloc=gc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <v32/memory.hpp>
.PP
.B namespace v32 {
.PP
.B  "int   arena_mark();"
.BI "void  arena_release( int " mark " );"
.B  "void  arena_reset();"
.B  "int   arena_used();"
.B  "int   arena_free();"
.PP
.B  "void  gc_collect();"
.B  "int   gc_used();"
.B  "int   gc_free();"
.B  "int   gc_collections();"
.PP
.B }
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Available only when the program is transpiled with
.B \-\-alloc=arena
or
.BR \-\-alloc=gc ;
including it otherwise is a compile-time error. The
.B arena_*
functions exist only with the first and the
.B gc_*
ones only with the second: calling one in the other mode does not compile. With
.B \-\-alloc=arena
every
.BR new ,
.B new[]
and
.B std::vector
buffer comes from one block (see
.BR \-\-heap\-words )
and nothing is freed one by one. All sizes are in 32-bit words.
.TP
.B arena_mark
An opaque mark of the arena's current top.
.TP
.B arena_release
Frees everything allocated since
.IR mark ,
after running the destructors of the objects still alive there, newest first.
An object already
.BR delete d
is not destroyed twice. Releasing an older mark invalidates newer ones.
.TP
.B arena_reset
Releases everything.
.TP
.B arena_used
Words in use.
.TP
.B arena_free
Words still free. When an allocation does not fit, the cartridge prints
"OUT OF MEMORY (arena full)" on the screen and halts.
.PP
With
.B \-\-alloc=gc
the same allocations come from one heap (see
.BR \-\-heap\-words )
that a conservative mark-sweep collector keeps clean, so nothing has to be
.BR delete d;
.B delete
still runs the destructor and frees at once, and an object that is collected gets no
destructor. The roots are the file-scope variables that can hold a pointer, the machine
stack and the registers; a word pointing anywhere inside a block keeps it alive. A
collection runs when an allocation finds no room (then it is retried), after
.B \-\-gc\-threshold
words were allocated since the last one (default a quarter of the heap), and on
.BR gc_collect .
.TP
.B gc_collect
Runs a full collection now. It is a pause: call it once per frame at a quiet point.
.TP
.B gc_used
Words in use (block headers included).
.TP
.B gc_free
Words not in use. When an allocation does not fit even after a collection, the cartridge
prints "OUT OF MEMORY (gc heap full)" on the screen and halts.
.TP
.B gc_collections
How many collections have run since the start of
.BR main .
.PP
An integer that looks like a heap address keeps a dead block alive; a pointer hidden from
the collector (xor-ed, or one past the end of its block) keeps nothing alive; raw
.B malloc
memory is not managed. Precise per-class pointer maps and finalizers are future work.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
.RS
void play_level()
{
    int level = v32::arena_mark();
    Enemy* boss = new Enemy( 3 );
    std::vector<int> path;          // grows inside the arena
    // ...
    v32::arena_release( level );    // boss's destructor runs; all is given back
}

// with --alloc=gc: no delete; once per frame, at a quiet point
void end_of_frame()
{
    v32::gc_collect();
}
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR v32c++ (1),
.BR misc.hpp (3v32)
.SH AUTHOR
Matthew Haas
